I am using python2.7 on CentOS 7, the latest version of scipy from pip.

def _unequal_var_ttest_denom(v1, n1, v2, n2):

When v2 (standard deviation from sample two passed from ttest_ind_from_stats) is 0, the context manager has no effect, and the code halts on a ZeroDivisionError exception. I've worked around it by modifying the code to catch the exception and assign df to np.nan, but not sure what should really be happening here.

_Original ticket http://projects.scipy.org/scipy/ticket/671 on 2008-05-21 by trac user gyroz, assigned to @wnbell._

The operators for division are wrong. The operator starting at line 34 should be:

```
    complex_wrapper operator/(const complex_wrapper& B) const {
      complex_wrapper result;
      c_type denom = 1.0 / (B.real * B.real + B.imag * B.imag);
      result.real = (npy_type::real * B.real + npy_type::imag * B.imag) * denom;
      result.imag = (npy_type::imag * B.real - npy_type::real * B.imag) * denom;
      return result;
    }
```

The operator starting at line 57 should accordingly be:

```
   complex_wrapper& operator/=(const complex_wrapper & B){
      c_type denom   = 1.0 / (B.real * B.real + B.imag * B.imag);
      c_type temp    = (npy_type::real * B.real + npy_type::imag * B.imag) * denom; 
      npy_type::imag = (npy_type::imag * B.real - npy_type::real * B.imag) * denom;
      npy_type::real = temp;
      return (*this);
    }
```

Best regards,
Sascha

_Original ticket http://projects.scipy.org/scipy/ticket/248 on 2006-08-14 by @alberts, assigned to unknown._

Valgrind 3.1.0 reports a memory leak when running the following code:

```
#!python
from numpy import mgrid, pi, sin, ogrid
from scipy.interpolate import interp2d
y, x = mgrid[0:pi:20j, 0:pi:21j]
z = sin(x+y)
I = interp2d(x, y, z)
```

The leak:

```
==31126== 48 (40 direct, 8 indirect) bytes in 1 blocks are definitely lost in loss record 11 of 40
==31126==    at 0x40051F9: malloc (vg_replace_malloc.c:149)
==31126==    by 0x4434AF2: array_alloc (arrayobject.c:6437)
==31126==    by 0x443C59A: PyArray_NewFromDescr (arrayobject.c:5063)
==31126==    by 0x445268D: PyArray_FromArray (arrayobject.c:7580)
==31126==    by 0x4452765: PyArray_FromAny (arrayobject.c:8030)
==31126==    by 0x5E6DB56: fitpack_surfit (__fitpack.h:152)
==31126==    by 0x5F15D2C: PyCFunction_Call (in /usr/lib/libpython2.4.so.1.0)
...
```

The following happens at `__fitpack.h`, line 152:

```
#!c
ap_wrk=(PyArrayObject *)PyArray_ContiguousFromObject(wrk_py, PyArray_DOUBLE, 0, 1);
```

However, on a success return around line 227, only the following variables are DECREF'ed:

```
#!c
Py_DECREF(ap_x);
Py_DECREF(ap_y); 
Py_DECREF(ap_z);             
Py_DECREF(ap_w);        
```

However, the error cleanup code does:

```
#!c
Py_XDECREF(ap_x);
Py_XDECREF(ap_y);
Py_XDECREF(ap_z);
Py_XDECREF(ap_w);
Py_XDECREF(ap_tx);
Py_XDECREF(ap_ty);
Py_XDECREF(ap_wrk);
```

I think one or more DECREF's is missing when doing a successful return.

Checked with !NumPy 1.0b3.dev3015 and !SciPy 0.5.0.2160.

Investigate/fix failure of `test_heequb` failure caused by gh-11664.
@Kai-Striega @ilayn feel free to push here directly.

@ilayn it seems that you understood the issue, but I don't, so I wanted to rule out the possibility that the addition of the `gejsv` tests are interfering somehow with the tests of `heequb` (workspace issues?). Then I'd confirm that removing the `gejsv` wrappers altogether fixes the failure.

This code looks wrong: https://github.com/scipy/scipy/blob/master/scipy/integrate/__quadpack.h#L128

```
  check = PyObject_GetAttrString(func, "argtypes");
  if (!PyTuple_Check(check) || (PyTuple_GET_SIZE(check) != 1) || 
      (PyTuple_GET_ITEM(check, 0) != c_double)) {
    if ((PyTuple_GET_ITEM(check, 0) == c_int) && (PyTuple_GET_ITEM(check, 1) == c_double)){   
      /*If first param is int and second is of double type, we have valid_multivariate */
       ...
       return Valid_Multivariate_Ctype;
    }
    else 
      goto fail;
  }
```

The argument checks are done wrong (PyTuple_GET_ITEM called even if PyTuple_Check fails, etc).

Moreover, the actual argument type used later on is a pointer to a double, not double. The check should check that.

The check at https://github.com/scipy/scipy/blob/master/scipy/integrate/__quadpack.h#L396 should check for equality, not inequality.

This code stores a pointer to a local variable, which is invalid: https://github.com/scipy/scipy/blob/master/scipy/integrate/quadpack.h#L176

The code stores pointers of different types in `global_args` and `storage->args`, e.g. https://github.com/scipy/scipy/blob/master/scipy/integrate/quadpack.h#L170. These variables should be `void*`, not `PyObject*`.

I didn't have time to look at the PR that added these at the time...

Currently the cdf-ppf round trip for small x return wrong values as shown here:
In [85]: truncexpon.ppf(truncexpon.cdf(1e-100,1),1)
Out[85]: 0.0

By replacing exp(x)-1 with expm1(x) and log(1+x) with log1p(x) one can improve the accuracy of the truncexpon distribution (_pdf, _logpdf, _cdf and _ppf) to machine precision for small x.

Replacing _pdf, _logpdf, _cdf and _ppf with

```
def _pdf(self, x, b):
    return exp(-x) / (-expm1(-b))

def _logpdf(self, x, b):
    return - x - log(-expm1(-b))

def _cdf(self, x, b):
    return expm1(-x) / expm1(-b)

def _ppf(self, q, b):
    return - log1p(q * expm1(-b))
```

one achieves 
In [92]: truncexpon.ppf(truncexpon.cdf(1e-100,1),1)
Out[92]: 1e-100

Currently the sf-isf round trip for large x return wrong values as shown here:
In [85]: rayleigh.isf(rayleigh.sf(9,1),1)
Out[85]: 9.0000752648062754

By replacing exp(x)-1 with expm1(x) and log(1+x) with log1p(x) one can improve the accuracy of the rayleigh distribution (_cdf, _sf, and _ppf) to machine precision for large x.

Replacing _cdf  and _ppf with

```
def _cdf(self, r):
    return - expm1(-r * r / 2.0)

def _sf(self, r):
    return exp(-r * r / 2.0)

def _isf(self, q):
    return sqrt(-2 * log(q))

def _ppf(self, q):
    return sqrt(-2 * log1p(-q))
```

one acheives 
In [86]: rayleigh.isf(rayleigh.sf(9,1),1)
Out[86]: 9.0

Currently the cdf-ppf round trip for small x return wrong values as shown here:
    In [85]: lomax.ppf(lomax.cdf(1e-100,1),1)
    Out[85]: 0.0

By replacing(1+x) with exp(log1p(x),  exp(x)-1 with expm1(x) and log(1+x) with log1p(x) one can improve the accuracy of the lomax distribution (_logpdf, _cdf, _sf, _logsf and _ppf) to machine precision for small x.

Replacing _logpdf, _cdf, _sf, logsf and _ppf with

```
def _logpdf(self, x, c):
    return log(c) - (c + 1) * log1p(x)

def _cdf(self, x, c):
    return -expm1(-c*log1p(x))

def _sf(self, x, c):
    return exp(-c*log1p(x))

def _logsf(self, x, c):
    return -c * log1p(x)

def _ppf(self, q, c):
    return expm1(-log1p(-q)/c)
```

one acheives 
    In [86]: lomax.ppf(lomax.cdf(1e-100,1),1)
    Out[86]: 1e-100

Currently the cdf-ppf round trip for small x return wrong values as shown here:
In [85]: gompertz.ppf(gompertz.cdf(1e-100,1),1)
Out[85]: 0.0

By replacing exp(x)-1 with expm1(x) and log(1+x) with log1p(x) one can improve the accuracy of the gompertz distribution (_logpdf, _cdf and _ppf)  to machine precision for small x.

Replacing  _logpdf, _cdf and _ppf with

```
def _logpdf(self, x, c):
    return log(c) + x - c * expm1(x)

def _cdf(self, x, c):
    return -expm1(-c * expm1(x))

def _ppf(self, q, c):
    return log1p(-1.0 / c * log1p(-q))
```

one achieves 
 In [92]: gompertz.ppf(gompertz.cdf(1e-100,1),1)
Out[92]: 1e-100

Currently the betaprime._cdf implementation is skipped due problems with special.hyp2f1. However, according to http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beta_prime_distribution the cdf is equal to betainc(a, b, x/(1.+x)).

Thus betaprime._cdf could be implemented as:

```
def _cdf(self, x, a, b):
    return special.betainc(a, b, x/(1.+x))
```

One solution is to replace _logpmf with:

```
def _logpmf(self, x, n, p):
    coeff = gamln(n + x) - gamln(x + 1) - gamln(n)
    return coeff + n*log(p) + special.xlog1py(x, -p)
```

Another issue is that according to wikipedia it is only defined for n>0, but here it is defined for n>=0.
Perhaps consider replacing _argcheck with

```
def _argcheck(self, n, p):
        return (n > 0) & (p >= 0) & (p <= 1)
```

This issue is an exact analog of [numpy 3733](https://github.com/numpy/numpy/issues/3733), because the code between these this function and `np.percentile` is mostly duplicated.

`scoreatpercentile` claims to function with the `per` argument (the percentiles to calculate) being either a scalar or an array_like. However, the initial lines of code do:

```
if per == 0:
    return a.min(axis=axis, out=out)
elif per == 100:
    return a.max(axis=axis, out=out)
```

which of course breaks with a "truth value of array undefined" exception if `per` is in fact an ndarray. It doesn't break if `per` is a list, since `per` isn't coerced to array-ness, but that's just two bugs canceling.

From an initial reading, I think the implementations of these two functions could be merged. `np.concatenate` could be given an `interpolate_method` argument like `scoreatpercentile`, and then `scipy.stats.scoreatpercentile` could just become an alias for the Numpy function.

_Original ticket http://projects.scipy.org/scipy/ticket/225 on 2006-07-07 by @brickZA, assigned to @wnbell._

I remember succesfully using integer dtypes with sparse matrices before. Now it seems only floating point types are supported:

{{
In [1]: import numpy

In [2]: from scipy import sparse

In [3]: mat = sparse.lil_matrix((3,3), dtype=numpy.int8)

In [4]: mat.dtype
Out[4]: dtype('<f8')
}}}

In my code I have a very sparse matrix consisting only of values in [-1, 0, 1]. Using int8 saves quite a bit of memory. Of course I'm not solving the matrix, only using its matvec() method.

scipy already has support for creating a random orthogonal matrix at https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/generated/scipy.stats.ortho_group.html .  For quantum applications a more common task is to create a random (complex valued) unitary matrix.  

In matlab this is done as follows:

```
function U = randU(n)
X = (randn(n) + i*randn(n))/sqrt(2);
[Q,R] = qr(X);
R = diag(diag(R)./abs(diag(R)));
U = Q*R;
```

It would be great if this functionality could be added to scipy.

Please consider adding these two wrappers to the `gaussian_kde` class for being consistent with the rest of the distributions in `scipy.stats`:

``` python
def pdf(self, x):
    return self.evaluate(x)
def logpdf(self, x):
    return numpy.log(self.evaluate(x))
```

A Gaussian KDE can be thought as a non-parametric probability distribution.

_Original ticket http://projects.scipy.org/scipy/ticket/1573 on 2011-12-08 by trac user trobinso, assigned to unknown._

While working with a multivariate information problem for a paper yet to be defined, I wrote a useful multivariate integrator for n integrations over n variables. The following is my rough code, as based on _dblquad_ and _tplquad_ in _scipy/integrate/quadpack.py_:

```
def _infuncn(x,func,gfun,hfun,count,epsabs,epsrel,more_args):
    a = gfun(x)
    b = hfun(x)
    myargs = (x,) + more_args
    if count == 1:
        return quad(func,a,b,args=myargs,epsabs=epsabs,epsrel=epsrel)[0]
    else:
        return quad(_infuncn,a,b,(func,a,b,count-1,epsabs,epsrel,args),epsabs=epsabs,epsrel=epsrel)

def nquad(func, a, b, args=(), epsabs=1.49e-8, epsrel=1.49e-8, count=2)
    __ Recursive, N-depth integrator with number of variables given __

    # Preconditions
    if not isinstance( count, int )
        raise TypeError('Variable "count" must be an integer value')
    if count < 2:
        raise ValueError('Variable "count" must be at least 2 to perform \
            meaningful multivariate integration. Consider using quad(...) instead.')

    return quad(_infuncn,a,b,(func,a,b,count-1,epsabs,epsrel,args),epsabs=epsabs,epsrel=epsrel)
```

This form makes several assumptions for simplicity's sake:
1) epsabs and epsrel are the same value for all integrations
2) a and b define static bounds for all integrations
3) In cases where fewer than two integrations are desired, _quad_ should be used instead.

It's a simple thing that I'd like to contribute back to the project. I expect it can be made more robust by your team; in its current form, it proves quite useful for n-interaction information (that is, n-term mutual information for every n+1).

My issue is about ...

#### Reproducing code example:

Code in kendalltau in see stats.py returns pvalue of 0 if sample size is >= 171 (this was introduced as a fix in #10056):

```
if xtie == 0 and ytie == 0 and method == 'exact':
        # Exact p-value, see Maurice G. Kendall, "Rank Correlation Methods" (4th Edition), Charles Griffin & Co., 1970.
        c = min(dis, tot-dis)
        if size <= 0:
            raise ValueError
        elif c < 0 or 2*c > size*(size-1):
            raise ValueError
        elif size == 1:
            pvalue = 1.0
        elif size == 2:
            pvalue = 1.0
        elif c == 0:
            pvalue = 2.0/math.factorial(size) if size < 171 else 0.0
        elif c == 1:
            pvalue = 2.0/math.factorial(size-1) if (size-1) < 171 else 0.0
        else:
            new = [0.0]*(c+1)
            new[0] = 1.0
            new[1] = 1.0
            for j in range(3,size+1):
                old = new[:]
                for k in range(1,min(j,c+1)):
                    new[k] += new[k-1]
                for k in range(j,c+1):
                    new[k] += new[k-1] - old[k-j]
            pvalue = 2.0*sum(new)/math.factorial(size) if size < 171 else 0.0
```

However, the p-value should follow a uniform distribution under the null hypothesis.

#### Error message:

None

#### Scipy/Numpy/Python version information:

Scipy >= 1.3.0

Follows my comment #13367. I added the tests that @WarrenWeckesser did on the PR. Instead of reimplementing hypergeom, I am using boost's c++ hypergeometric distrubution implementation.

